fix(scripts): stop reading a successful match as a breach

The protected-surfaces gate failed twice on CI with

    printf: write error: Broken pipe
    FAIL: the Makefile no longer builds a binary named innsegl

and the Makefile was correct both times. Two pull requests were re-run for it,
and it was written off once as a stale merge base and once as environmental.
Neither was true.

`grep -q` exits the moment it matches and closes its end of the pipe. The writer
is still writing, takes EPIPE, and under `set -o pipefail` the pipeline's status
is non-zero -- so the gate reads a SUCCESSFUL match as a failed one. The Makefile
is 40 KB and BINARY is on line six, so grep is finished after a couple of hundred
bytes with the rest still to come. Whether that survives is a question about the
pipe buffer, which is a question about the machine: it passed here every time and
failed there.

Both checks now feed grep from a here-doc. There is no second process to close
anything, so the failure mode is gone rather than made less likely.

No new test. The gate's own selftest already mutates this surface and watches it
breach; what was missing was never a case, it was a pipeline. Verified by
renaming the binary and watching the refusal, then restoring it -- and the
selftest's eleven phases still pass.

The same printf-into-grep-q shape appears in three selftests over much smaller
strings. Left alone deliberately: changing them would be churn on code that
cannot reach the buffer size where this bites.

